# TypeScript & React conventions (client)

## Style

- Exports first, then private types, constants and helpers. The reader sees
  what the file is for on the first screen.
- Exception: the props type of the file's main component goes right above
  that component, even when the type isn't exported.
- Always brace `if` / `for` / `while` bodies, including early returns.
- Functions are verbs (`parseStorageUrl`); components are nouns.
- A direct mapping is a `Record`:
  `const zoneRank: Record<Zone, number> = { visible: 0, … }`. Use a
  function when the result is computed, not looked up.
- API functions start with `get` / `fetch` / `create` / … (`getHealth`,
  `getListStorage`), not a bare Rust-style verb like `listStorage`.
- Components and one-expression helpers are arrow functions:
  `export const StoragePage = () => { … }`.
- A one-statement arrow whose result must be discarded can use `void` and
  stay an expression: `() => void map.delete(id)`.
- `null` for "no value", not `undefined`. Use `== null` / truthiness checks
  unless `null` and `undefined` genuinely mean different things.
- No `!` non-null assertions to appease the checker — restructure instead.
  (The only accepted one is `document.getElementById("root")!` in `main.tsx`.)
- No `readonly` without a real contract behind it.
- Descriptive names; one-letter names only for loop counters and math.
- Event handlers are `onX`, not `handleX`.
- `{@link Foo}` in JSDoc, not rustdoc-style `` [`Foo`] ``.
- Formatting is oxfmt with `client/.oxfmtrc.json` (double quotes, 80
  columns, sorted imports) — don't hand-format. Run it from the root as
  `npm run format -- <paths relative to client/>`. Never bare
  `npx oxfmt`: outside `client/` it silently falls back to defaults.

## API layer

- All network calls go through `client/src/api/core.ts` (`apiGet`). Never
  call `fetch` from components.
- Per area: `api/<area>.io.ts` holds zod schemas and the inferred types,
  `api/<area>.ts` holds the functions (`getListStorage`, `getHealth`).
- The wire is snake_case; `core.ts` snake_cases query keys and camelizes
  responses. Everything outside `core.ts` is camelCase only — no
  `is_dir` in TS code.
- Errors are typed (`NetworkError`, `HttpError`, `DecodeError`). Don't
  catch generically and swallow; show the failure or let it propagate.

## React

- `react` is `@faiwer/react` (aliased in `package.json`). It lacks some
  newer APIs (`use`, `useOptimistic`, …): check before relying on one.
  Third-party React libraries are pinned to versions already proven in
  `~/cu/tengri-xc/client`.
- Every component is memoized by default (`memo` is not in the API). A child
  re-renders when a prop fails `Object.is`.
- Loading data: `useAsyncData((signal) => apiFn(…, { signal }), deps)` from
  `core/hooks`. Other async effects: `useAsyncEffect`. Both own the
  `AbortController`; don't hand-roll `useEffect` + `.then`.
- Deps of those hooks aren't linted: list what should re-run the effect,
  not everything the closure reads.
- Never list a ref in an effect dependency array. A ref is not reactive, so
  `.current` changing does not re-run the effect. Read `.current` inside
  `useAsyncEffect`: its deps aren't linted, so the ref is not demanded.
- When the ref is attached on every render, an effect sees `.current` set.
  Pass it through `nullthrows` (`core/helpers/nullthrows`). When the node
  renders only under a condition, store the element in `useState`: state
  updates re-run the effect as the node mounts and unmounts.
- Suffix a hook with `X` when `nullthrows` is inside it, so the result is
  never `null`. Leave the suffix off when the value is optional and `null`
  is a real result.
- A context and the hooks that read it share one file. `PreviewQueueContext`
  and `usePreviewQueueContextX` both live in `context.ts`.
- Errors nobody handles go to `trackError` (`core/errors`), not `console`.
- No reflexive `useCallback` / `useMemo`.
- Conditional or combined class names go through `clsx`:
  `clsx(styles.root, active && styles.active, className)`, not template
  strings or `[…].filter(Boolean).join(" ")`.
- Pages live in `pages/`, routes are declared in `App.tsx`.
- Use `tsx` extension for every file that might contain React nodes.
- Do not keep in one .tsx files too much code. Instead move medium+-sized
  subcomponents into separate files.
- If the new component would take 200+ LoC of TSX code consider creating a
  separate folder for it.

## Checks

`npm run typecheck` (tsrs), `npx --no -- oxlint --deny-warnings client` and
`npm run format:check -w client` must pass.
